    "lines": [
      {
        "scene": "hook",
        "text": "Your tax-free £12,570 never moves. So why does your tax go up?",
        "start": 0.27,
        "seconds": 5.25,
        "window": 5.6,
        "fill": 0.938
      },
      {
        "scene": "headline",
        "text": "With a UK Budget coming, here's the part that hits every payslip. Up to £12,570, you pay no income tax.",
        "start": 5.87,
        "seconds": 7.78,
        "window": 8.4,
        "fill": 0.926
      },
      {
        "scene": "rate",
        "text": "Above it, the basic rate takes 20% of every extra pound.",
        "start": 14.27,
        "seconds": 3.39,
        "window": 3.87,
        "fill": 0.876
      },
      {
        "scene": "road",
        "text": "Say your pay of £30,000 rises with prices, an assumed 3% a year. In 5 years, it's £34,778.",
        "start": 18.13,
        "seconds": 9.18,
        "window": 9.73,
        "fill": 0.943
      },
      {
        "scene": "frozen",
        "text": "But if the allowance stays at £12,570, it falls behind. Kept up with prices, it would reach £14,572.",
        "start": 27.87,
        "seconds": 9.35,
        "window": 9.93,
        "fill": 0.942
      },
      {
        "scene": "drag",
        "text": "So more of the same real pay gets taxed. That's fiscal drag.",
        "start": 37.8,
        "seconds": 3.47,
        "window": 3.8,
        "fill": 0.913
      },
      {
        "scene": "outro",
        "text": "On Budget day, watch the thresholds, not just the rates.",
        "start": 41.6,
